## What changed and why

**Root cause of the failure:** Salesforce enforces a hard governor limit of **50 `System.enqueueJob` calls per Apex transaction**. A nightly API close of 200 cases fires the trigger once with all 200 records in `Trigger.new`; the original code tried to call `enqueueJob` 200 times in that single transaction → `LimitException` at job #51.

**Fix – introduce a Batch Apex dispatcher (`CaseSurveyDispatcher`):**

| Concern | How it is handled |
|---|---|
| 50-enqueue-per-transaction limit | `Database.executeBatch(…, 50)` guarantees each `execute()` call receives **at most 50** cases, so each batch chunk enqueues ≤ 50 Queueable jobs. |
| Isolation per case | The Queueable still processes **one case per job** (`CaseSurveyJob` inserted into a single DML). A failure on case *A* cannot affect case *B*. |
| Trigger stays bulk-safe | The trigger does no DML and no enqueuing; it only builds a list and hands it to the batch. This keeps the trigger within its own 50-`enqueueJob` budget (it makes **zero** such calls). |
| Small-volume path (agents closing 1–5 cases from a list view) | The batch is still used; the overhead is negligible because Batch Apex is fully asynchronous. No need for a separate "if ≤ 50 enqueue directly" branch that would re-introduce a maintenance hazard. |
| Future vendor integration (callout) | When HTTP callouts are added to `CaseSurveyJob`, it simply gains `implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts`—the dispatcher and trigger need no change. |

**No other files modified.** `CaseSurveyJob` logic is unchanged from the original answer (re-query, null-contact guard, single `insert Task`).

The error `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'Name')` is caused by the template expression `{account.Owner.Name}`: the getter `account` returns `this.accountRecord?.data`, which is the top-level `Record` object — it has `fields` and `apiName` but **no** `Owner` property directly on it. Accessing `.Owner.Name` on `undefined` throws the TypeError. The fix is to replace direct nested accesses in the template with **individual getters** that safely traverse the correct `data.fields.*` structure.

